ing side of an OUTER JOIN. This specific filtering effectively mandates that there's a matching row in the non-preserving side's table, thus allowing the OUTER JOIN to be converted to an INNER JOIN for efficiency.\n**Transformations**: Convert OUTER JOINS to INNER JOINS when followed by WHERE clauses that eliminate the possibility of NULL results from the non-preserving side. For example, `SELECT * FROM A LEFT OUTER JOIN B ON condition1 WHERE B.column IS NOT NULL` can be efficiently rewritten as `SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON condition1` because the WHERE clause enforces a logic that requires a match in B for every row returned.\n```\n\nLogical Plan Changes After Rewrite: ```\n  LogicalSort(sort0=[$4(ss_ext_sales_price)], sort1=[$1(i_brand)], sort2=[$0(i_brand_id)], sort3=[$2(i_manufact_id)], sort4=[$3(i_manufact)], dir0=[DESC], dir1=[ASC], dir2=[ASC], dir3=[ASC], dir4=[ASC], fetch=[100])\n    LogicalProject(brand_id=[$1(i_brand_id)], brand=[$0(i_brand)], i_manufact_id=[$2(i_manufact_id)], i_manufact=[$3(i_manufact)], ext_price=[$4(ss_ext_sales_price)])\n      LogicalAggregate(group=[{0, 1, 2, 3}], ext_price=[SUM($4)])\n        LogicalProject(brand=[$59(i_brand)], brand_id=[$58(i_brand_id)], i_manufact_id=[$64(i_manufact_id)], i_manufact=[$65(i_manufact)], ss_ext_sales_price=[$43(ss_ext_sales_price)])\n-         LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($0(d_date_sk), $28(ss_sold_date_sk)), =($30(ss_item_sk), $51(i_item_sk)), =($31(ss_customer_sk), $73(c_customer_sk)), =($77(c_current_addr_sk), $91(ca_address_sk)), =($35(ss_store_sk), $104(s_store_sk)), =(CAST($63(i_category)):CHAR(6), 'Sports'), =($6(d_year), 1999), =($8(d_moy), 3), <>(SUBSTRING($100(ca_zip), 1, 5), SUBSTRING($129(s_zip), 1, 5)), =(CAST($99(ca_state)):CHAR(2), 'OK'), =($85(c_birth_month), 11), >=($39(ss_wholesale_cost), 71), <=($39(ss_wholesale_cost), 91))])\n-           LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n+         LogicalJoin(condition=[AND(=($35(ss_store_sk), $104(s_store_sk)), <>(SUBSTRING($100(ca_zip), 1, 5), SUBSTRING($129(s_zip), 1, 5)))], joinType=[inner])\n+           LogicalJoin(condition=[=($77(c_current_addr_sk), $91(ca_address_sk))], joinType=[inner])\n-             LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n+             LogicalJoin(condition=[=($31(ss_customer_sk), $73(c_customer_sk))], joinType=[inner])\n?                                    ++++++++++++ +++ +++++++++++++ ++++ ++++++\n\n-               LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n?                                       --\n\n+               LogicalJoin(condition=[=($30(ss_item_sk), $51(i_item_sk))], joinType=[inner])\n?                                      ++++++++++  ++++++++++++++++++++++\n\n-                 LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n?                                         --\n\n+                 LogicalJoin(condition=[=($0(d_date_sk), $28(ss_sold_date_sk))], joinType=[inner])\n?                                        +++++++++  +++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n\n-                   LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n+                   LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($6(d_year), 1999), =($8(d_moy), 3))])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[date_dim]])\n+                   LogicalFilter(condition=[SEARCH($11(ss_wholesale_cost), Sarg[[71..91]])])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[store_sales]])\n+                 LogicalFilter(condition=[=(CAST($12(i_category)):CHAR(6), 'Sports')])\n                    LogicalTableScan(table=[[item]])\n+               LogicalFilter(condition=[=($12(c_birth_month), 11)])\n                  LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer]])\n+             LogicalFilter(condition=[=(CAST($8(ca_state)):CHAR(2), 'OK')])\n                LogicalTableScan(table=[[customer_address]])\n-             LogicalTableScan(table=[[store]])\n? --\n\n+           LogicalTableScan(table=[[store]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': "Step 1: The logical plan changes after rewrite indicate that the query rewrite rule has applied to the given SQL query. The changes involve moving filter conditions into the ON clause of INNER JOIN operations and transforming OUTER JOINS with subsequent WHERE clauses that filter columns from the non-preserving side.\n\nStep 2: Parsing the logical plan changes, we can identify the following detailed changes:\n\n- The original query had multiple INNER JOIN operations without any filter conditions in the ON clause. After applying the rewrite rule, some of these filter conditions have been moved into the ON clause.\n- A new INNER JOIN operation has been added between the store_sales and item tables based on the ss_item_sk column.\n- Another new INNER JOIN operation has been added between the customer and customer_address tables based on the c_current_addr_sk column.\n\nStep 3: The query rewrite rule contains three cases. We can match these cases to the parsed SQL query changes as follows:\n\nCase 1: This case applies when a filter condition is placed on the result set of an INNER JOIN operation, but the filter's expressions do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join. In our rewritten logical plan, we see that some filter conditions have been moved into the ON clause of INNER JOIN operations (e.g., =($0(d_date_sk), $28(ss_sold_date_sk)), =($30(ss_item_sk), $51(i_item_sk)), etc.). This matches Case 1 of the query rewrite rule.\n\nCase 2: This case applies when a filter condition applies to the result set of a LEFT or RIGHT OUTER JOIN and the filter applies solely to columns from the non-preserving side of the join. We do not see any OUTER JOINS in our rewritten logical plan, so this case does not apply.\n\nCase 3: This case applies when a subsequent WHERE clause filter mandates the presence of non-NULL values in columns from the non-preserving side of an OUTER JOIN. Since we do not have any OUTER JOINS in our rewritten logical plan, this case also does not apply.\n\nStep 4: Based on the matched cases and parsed SQL query changes, we can explain how the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query as follows:\n\nThe query rewrite rule has applied Case 1 of the rule to move filter conditions into the ON clause of INNER JOIN operations. This is evident from the rewritten logical plan, where some filter conditions have been moved into the ON clause of INNER JOIN operations (e.g., =($0(d_date_sk), $28(ss_sold_date_sk)), =($30(ss_item_sk), $51(i_item_sk)), etc.). This transformation can improve query performance by reducing the size of datasets before the join occurs.", 'time': 11.939124822616577}
